Lucky for us, Go's `encoding/json` package actually implements float64 marshalling the same way as the ECMAScript spec. In `floatEncoder`:

```go
	// Convert as if by ES6 number to string conversion.
	// This matches most other JSON generators.
	// See golang.org/issue/6384 and golang.org/issue/14135.
	// Like fmt %g, but the exponent cutoffs are different
	// and exponents themselves are not padded to two digits.
```

This is exactly the difference we saw with `%g`.

This PR reimplements our own suspicious conversions using `encoding/json`.

The tests are extensive, including tests pulled (with attribution) from `ryu`, the floating point printing implementation used in Go. V8 uses `Grisu3`, which Go also used to use. Regardless, the behavior is identical, and a load of testing shows it.

There's also a test which sends every test over to `node` and then verifies that things work identically, along with a fuzz test which attempts to find any place that differs with node. I have found no such difference in a bunch of testing. I even tried `bun`; it all checks out.

If this doesn't work in the future, then we'll definitely need to go port ryu/grisu3/dragonbox or something, which is a bad time. Go's json library basically already does this, applying the correct fixups, so I think this is solid.

---

For _parsing_ numbers, the ECMAScript spec is written in terms of a grammar, which is more or less the same as a JS parser, which is a problem.

To tackle this, I've instead written something which effectively "cleans up" the user's input to then provide to Go's existing number parsing functions; extensive fuzz testing found bugs, but the current implementation has been fuzzed for a pretty long time with good success.

